  "summary": "EFF reviews California's 2026 legislative session, celebrating wins on workplace AI surveillance and youth digital literacy while mourning losses on consumer privacy, CIPA, and ALPR regulation. Notably, SB 1013, which would have regulated ALPRs, stalled after late amendments doubled retention periods; EFF and partners opposed the weakened version and note no ALPR regulation bill has passed in California since 2022.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "California lawmakers have lagged in enacting meaningful limits on automated license plate readers with all proposed ALPR regulation bills failing to pass since 2022.",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    },
    {
      "quote": "The final draft would have doubled the current data retention period and allowed agencies to archive rather than delete data unrelated to active investigations\u2014effectively shifting away from data minimization toward indefinite retention.",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    },
    {
      "quote": "Next session, we expect ALPR legislation to be top of mind as states across the country reckon with the harms the technology has caused.",
      "paragraph_idx": 41
    }
  ],
